  "title": "Erdős Problem #680",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Is it true that, for all sufficiently large $n$, there exists some $k$ such that $ p(n+k)>k^2+1, $ where $p(m)$ denotes the least prime factor of $m$?\nCan one prove this is false if we replace $k^2+1$ by $e^{(1+\\epsilon)\\sqrt{k}}+C_\\epsilon$, for all $\\epsilon>0$, where $C_\\epsilon>0$ is some constant?",
